     1	/**
     2	 * panelActivation.ts — when each /next panel is allowed to load its data.
     3	 *
     4	 * PURE: no backend call, no Svelte, no DOM. It only decides *whether* and
     5	 * *with which project* a panel's loader may run; the loaders themselves are
     6	 * handed in (see `shellPanels.ts` for the real ones).
     7	 *
     8	 * The rule the shell is built on is "nothing loads at launch", and there are
     9	 * two different launch noises to keep out:
    10	 *
    11	 * 1. **The center dock announcing a tab.** Building or restoring the tab area
    12	 *    reports an active tab, and that report is indistinguishable from a click.
    13	 *    So tab loads stay switched off until `allowPanelLoads()` is called, which
    14	 *    the page does one timer tick after the frame is ready — every one of
    15	 *    dockview's own announcements is delivered by then (they travel on
    16	 *    microtasks, which all drain before the first timer callback). What the
    17	 *    announcement still does, even switched off, is tell this file the tab is
    18	 *    on screen: the tab loads nothing at launch, and the session picked next is
    19	 *    what points it at a project.
    20	 * 2. **Start-up re-attaching a terminal.** Re-attaching a session that survived
    21	 *    a reload selects it, which looks exactly like the user picking it. So
    22	 *    session loads stay switched off until `allowSessionLoads()` is called,
    23	 *    which the page does when start-up has finished.
    24	 *
    25	 * After that, loading is driven by user actions only: bringing a tab to the
    26	 * front, picking a session, and opening a view of the tool column. A panel that
    27	 * has never been shown never loads, and changing session re-loads only the
    28	 * panels the user has actually opened — and of those, only the ones the change
    29	 * tells something new. A panel is already showing the folder the session you
    30	 * just picked is in when that session shares a project or a checkout with the
    31	 * one you left, and reading it again would throw the panel's work away and
    32	 * rebuild the same answer. So each panel remembers the folder it was last
    33	 * loaded for, and a pick that does not change it loads nothing.
    34	 *
    35	 * Four panels are neither a tab nor always on screen: **source control**, the
    36	 * **worktree manager**, the **stacks pane** and the **context cards**. All four
    37	 * live in the tool column on the right, as four of the five views its icon strip
    38	 * switches between, and the shell opens on none of them. So all four follow the
    39	 * same principle by a third route — they load when you can actually see them.
    40	 * Picking a session loads one only while it is in view, and opening one loads it
    41	 * if a session is already picked. Out of view they cost nothing; in view they
    42	 * are never stale.
    43	 *
    44	 * What counts as "in view" is not decided here — the tool column knows both
    45	 * halves of it (which view is open, and whether the pane inside is folded) and
    46	 * reports the answer as one yes or no, per view.
    47	 *
    48	 * The **Problems panel** in the bottom dock has the same route and a harder
    49	 * problem: the dock is on screen from the moment the shell opens, so "in view"
    50	 * cannot mean "mounted" there. `problemsVisible(true)` must only ever be
    51	 * reported from something the user did. Nothing in the shell reports it today —
    52	 * the panel's own Refresh button is the only way in — but the route is here and
    53	 * tested, so the day the dock grows tabs or a fold, one call is all it takes.
    54	 */
