     1	/**
     2	 * worktreesBackend.ts — the desktop commands the worktree manager needs, and
     3	 * nothing else.
     4	 *
     5	 * This lane owns its own wrappers (the wave rule for a parallel lane), written
     6	 * the same way `tauriSource.ts` writes its own: import `@tauri-apps/api/core`
     7	 * only inside the call, and answer `null` when we are not running inside the
     8	 * desktop app. `null` means "nothing was invoked" — the pane then says the
     9	 * plain truth about where this data lives instead of inventing any.
    10	 *
    11	 * Three of the five calls already exist in `tauriSource.ts` and are re-used from
    12	 * there. Two do not:
    13	 *
    14	 *  - **removing a worktree the destructive way.** The existing wrapper cannot
    15	 *    send `force`, and this pane's whole point is the button that can. So the
    16	 *    remove is written out here, and `force: true` is put on the wire ONLY when
    17	 *    the caller explicitly asks for it.
    18	 *  - **asking the desktop app what it can do.** An older build of the desktop
    19	 *    app has a `remove_project_worktree` command with no `force` on it at all.
    20	 *    Sending `force: true` to that build does NOT fail: the extra field is
    21	 *    dropped on the way in and an ordinary, careful remove happens instead —
    22	 *    which would refuse, while our screen claimed it had forced. There is no
    23	 *    way to tell those two builds apart from the outcome, so the app is asked
    24	 *    outright: `read_backend_capabilities` lists what this build supports, and
    25	 *    the forced remove is offered only when `worktreeForceRemove` is in that
    26	 *    list. A build too old to even have that command answers with an error,
    27	 *    which is caught here and read as "it cannot".
